<|channel>thought
<channel|>High digital checkout numbers on apps like Libby are often hailed as a triumph for public libraries. But is a "checkout" actually a win?

A user might download ten thrillers in a week only to finish one before the loan expires or interest fades. If we prioritize clicks over completion, we mistake browsing for reading. To truly measure success, we must look beyond the digital button press and start tracking deep engagement. We need to measure what actually sticks, not just what gets downloaded. [end of text]


